The '''Organització d'Energia Nuclear de Balboa''' (OENB) is a [[state-owned enterprise]] responsible for nuclear power generation in [[Balboa]]. It operates the country's first and only [[nuclear power plant]], the [[Central Nuclear de Sant Feliu]], located at [[Sant Feliu]] in the [[Tossal (Comarca)|Tossal]] of [[Jala Province|Jala]]. The plant supplies approximately 25 per cent of Balboa's electricity needs.

OENB was established in 1998 with the support of the [[Adonian Community]] International Cooperation for Renewable Energy programme and the [[Government of Balisca]], which provided technical expertise and financing for the construction of the plant. [[Uranium]] fuel is imported primarily from [[Balisca]].

== History ==

=== Background ===

Balboa's energy mix has historically been dominated by [[hydroelectricity]] from the dams of the [[Yalabirga]] range and by petroleum-based generation linked to the [[CIEA]] refining complex at [[Badia Curta]]. By the 1990s, the Balboan government faced two converging pressures. First, rising domestic electricity demand — driven by the expansion of the Badia Curta industrial complex, the growth of the canal corridor economy, and the broader industrialisation strategy — was expected to outstrip the capacity of hydropower alone, particularly during dry seasons when river flows declined. Second, growing public concern about air pollution in Balboa's cities — especially in the canal corridor and the Sant Cristòfor metropolitan area, where petroleum-based generation and industrial emissions were degrading air quality — had made the environmental costs of the existing energy mix politically untenable.

The '''1996 Adonian Community Edict on a Green Transition for the New Millennium''' created a further impetus. The edict established a framework under which AC member states could secure concessional loans for the construction of low-carbon power generation, including nuclear, hydroelectric, and other renewable sources. The Balboan government, which had been considering its options for diversifying electricity supply, seized the opportunity to access AC financing for a nuclear power programme.

Nuclear power was identified as the most suitable complement to hydropower: it would provide stable [[baseload]] electricity independent of rainfall, reduce dependence on petroleum-based generation, lower urban air pollution, and support the energy-intensive chemical and aluminium industries of Jala province. The alternative — expanding hydroelectric capacity through additional dams on the Yalabirga rivers — was the subject of intense public debate. Environmental groups and [[Birú people|Birú]] communities, whose traditional lands in the Yalabirga highlands had already been affected by existing dam construction, opposed further hydroelectric development on the grounds that it would entail additional land destruction, deforestation, and displacement of indigenous communities. The opposition made large-scale hydroelectric expansion politically unfeasible, strengthening the case for nuclear. However, Balboa lacked any domestic nuclear expertise, uranium resources, or regulatory framework.

=== Establishment (1998) ===

OENB was established in 1998 as a state-owned enterprise under the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources. The project was made possible through two channels of external cooperation:

* The '''[[Adonian Community]] International Cooperation for Renewable Energy''' programme, established under the 1996 edict, which provided technical assistance, safety standards, regulatory frameworks, and concessional financing for the construction of the plant. The programme was designed to support AC member states in developing civilian nuclear capacity under AC safety and non-proliferation oversight.

* The '''[[Government of Balisca]]''', which provided additional financing, engineering expertise, and a long-term uranium supply agreement. Balisca, a major uranium producer, had an interest in supporting Balboa's energy diversification as part of the broader Balboan-Baliscan strategic relationship, which also included the CIEA–COBAL oil supply arrangement.

The decision to locate the plant at Sant Feliu in the Tossal Comarca of Jala province was driven by several factors: the province's existing industrial infrastructure, its distance from major population centres, the availability of cooling water from the Shendan Ocean using a once-through cooling system, and the proximity to the Badia Curta industrial complex, which was the largest single consumer of electricity in the country.

=== Construction and operation ===

Construction of the Sant Feliu plant began in 2001 and was completed in 2009. The plant was built to AC safety standards under the oversight of the Adonian Community nuclear regulatory body. The first reactor achieved criticality in 2009 and began commercial operation in 2010.

The plant has operated without major incidents since commissioning. It is subject to regular inspections by both the Balboan nuclear regulatory authority and the AC nuclear safety inspectorate.

== Operations ==

* '''Headquarters''': [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]
* '''Plant''': [[Central Nuclear de Sant Feliu]]
* '''Plant location''': [[Sant Feliu]], [[Tossal (Comarca)|Tossal]], [[Jala Province|Jala]]
* '''Capacity''': 2 reactors, 2,800 MW total
* '''Output''': approximately 25 per cent of Balboa's electricity supply
* '''Employees''': approximately 1,600 (2026)
* '''Fuel source''': Uranium imported primarily from [[Balisca]]

The Sant Feliu plant provides baseload electricity to the national grid, complementing the hydropower from the Yalabirga dams and the petroleum-based generation linked to the Badia Curta complex. The plant's output is particularly important during dry periods, when hydropower capacity is reduced.

== Fuel supply ==

Balboa does not possess domestic uranium reserves. Uranium fuel for the Sant Feliu plant is imported primarily from [[Balisca]] under a long-term supply agreement established as part of the original bilateral cooperation framework. The arrangement is part of the broader Balboan-Baliscan energy relationship, which also includes the CIEA–COBAL oil supply contract under which CIEA supplies approximately 30 per cent of Balisca's oil consumption.

The fuel cycle is managed entirely within the AC framework: uranium is mined and processed in Balisca, fabricated into fuel assemblies at an AC-certified facility, and shipped to Balboa under AC non-proliferation safeguards. Spent fuel is stored on-site at Sant Feliu in purpose-built storage facilities, pending a long-term disposal solution under the AC nuclear waste management programme.

== Ownership ==

OENB is a state-owned enterprise wholly owned by the [[Government of Balboa]]. The company is not listed on any stock exchange. Its operations are overseen by the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources and subject to AC nuclear safety regulation.

== Planned second plant ==

In the mid-2020s, OENB announced plans for a second nuclear power plant to further reduce Balboa's dependence on thermal generation and meet growing electricity demand. The project is in the early planning stages, with site selection, environmental assessment, and AC financing negotiations underway. The proposed plant would be built and operated under the same AC safety and non-proliferation framework as the [[Central Nuclear de Sant Feliu]].

